WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.903
- We don't know exactly what causes love.

00:00:02.903 --> 00:00:05.185
Love is a mysterious thing as we know

00:00:05.185 --> 00:00:07.237
and how many plays have
been written about love,

00:00:07.237 --> 00:00:08.958
poems or books.

00:00:08.958 --> 00:00:12.665
(instrumental music)

00:00:12.665 --> 00:00:15.101
A person doesn't sleep, he doesn't eat,

00:00:15.101 --> 00:00:16.628
and becomes fixated or obsessed

00:00:16.628 --> 00:00:18.034
with another person.

00:00:18.034 --> 00:00:20.465
When it gets as severe as a disorder,

00:00:20.465 --> 00:00:23.700
it's very debilitating.

00:00:23.700 --> 00:00:25.303
Obsessions.

00:00:25.303 --> 00:00:28.386
(instrumental music)

00:00:32.425 --> 00:00:35.795
- [Narrator] Our expert
psychiatrist, Dr. Carol Berman

00:00:35.795 --> 00:00:38.003
explores obsession in the brain

00:00:38.003 --> 00:00:41.920
with the help of the new
Hulu original, Chance.

00:00:43.926 --> 00:00:47.013
- Obsession is when a person
has persistent thoughts

00:00:47.013 --> 00:00:49.639
about anything and they
keep thinking about it

00:00:49.639 --> 00:00:50.953
over and over again,

00:00:50.953 --> 00:00:53.800
as opposed to compulsions,
which are actual behaviors

00:00:53.800 --> 00:00:56.304
where a person does something
over and over again.

00:00:56.304 --> 00:00:58.779
- Mind keeps going back to Afghanistan.

00:00:58.779 --> 00:01:00.320
Man does like his scalps.

00:01:00.320 --> 00:01:02.022
Man does like his scalps.

00:01:02.022 --> 00:01:03.701
- Let's say you have to
arrange all the spoons

00:01:03.701 --> 00:01:06.275
in your drawer in a certain way

00:01:06.275 --> 00:01:08.135
and it takes you like three hours,

00:01:08.135 --> 00:01:10.223
and then punish yourself with thoughts

00:01:10.223 --> 00:01:12.036
like oh I'm bad, I did this wrong,

00:01:12.036 --> 00:01:13.629
I did this wrong, I did this wrong

00:01:13.629 --> 00:01:15.482
and the person is trying to fight it

00:01:15.482 --> 00:01:18.601
and they can't even get
out of the apartment.

00:01:18.601 --> 00:01:20.083
(instrumental music)

00:01:20.083 --> 00:01:23.588
I had one patient who was
obsessed with her girlfriend

00:01:23.588 --> 00:01:26.211
and she kept going after her,

00:01:26.211 --> 00:01:28.556
so erotomania is a little different

00:01:28.556 --> 00:01:30.407
and might be more
exciting because it has a

00:01:30.407 --> 00:01:33.257
sexual erotic element,
which is more exciting

00:01:33.257 --> 00:01:36.108
than arranging your spoons
in the drawer (laughing)

00:01:36.108 --> 00:01:39.483
but erotomania is when a person

00:01:39.483 --> 00:01:41.096
fixates on another person,

00:01:41.096 --> 00:01:43.296
constant emails to the person,

00:01:43.296 --> 00:01:45.613
telephone calls, stalking the person,

00:01:45.613 --> 00:01:48.284
dreaming about the person.

00:01:48.284 --> 00:01:50.609
- Jaclyn.

00:01:50.609 --> 00:01:52.606
Jaclyn.

00:01:52.606 --> 00:01:53.504
Jaclyn.

00:01:53.504 --> 00:01:55.502
(dishes clattering)

00:01:55.502 --> 00:01:57.442
- They could become depressed from it

00:01:57.442 --> 00:01:59.409
or the person could be enraged,

00:01:59.409 --> 00:02:01.850
so angry because the erotic object

00:02:01.850 --> 00:02:03.830
doesn't return their love.

00:02:03.830 --> 00:02:05.526
- He told me

00:02:05.526 --> 00:02:07.117
how it would be

00:02:07.117 --> 00:02:08.666
if I tried to leave.

00:02:08.666 --> 00:02:11.291
- In any medical field, if
you get very emotionally

00:02:11.291 --> 00:02:12.392
involved with the patient,

00:02:12.392 --> 00:02:14.155
you won't be able to help the person.

00:02:14.155 --> 00:02:16.255
We can't step outside the boundaries

00:02:16.255 --> 00:02:18.142
because people have done these things

00:02:18.142 --> 00:02:21.300
and terrible results have happened.

00:02:21.300 --> 00:02:24.193
Patients sometimes kill
themselves if the doctor starts

00:02:24.193 --> 00:02:27.579
hanging out with them or
trying to have sex with them,

00:02:27.579 --> 00:02:31.332
and the restrictions are very severe.

00:02:31.332 --> 00:02:33.578
- I want to help you,
I'm trying to help you.

00:02:33.578 --> 00:02:35.595
I care about you.

00:02:35.595 --> 00:02:37.950
- A lot of psychiatrists
will go for therapy.

00:02:37.950 --> 00:02:40.567
I've had doctors come to
me and try to, you know,

00:02:40.567 --> 00:02:44.265
stop erotomania or stop
obsessive compulsive disorder.

00:02:44.265 --> 00:02:46.242
We want to make sure we're okay.

00:02:46.242 --> 00:02:48.275
We're dealing with
mental patients everyday

00:02:48.275 --> 00:02:50.495
so some of us are not okay.

00:02:50.495 --> 00:02:53.172
Maybe we don't start okay or
why do we go into the field

00:02:53.172 --> 00:02:55.034
in the first place.

00:02:55.034 --> 00:02:58.166
- Familiar with the
concept of the Frozen Lake?

00:02:58.166 --> 00:03:00.443
Something you want so badly,

00:03:00.443 --> 00:03:03.703
but go out to the center
of Frozen Lake to reach it.

00:03:03.703 --> 00:03:05.292
- Where the ice is thinnest.

00:03:05.292 --> 00:03:07.641
- But you won't see that.

00:03:07.641 --> 00:03:09.305
- We're just at the beginning

00:03:09.305 --> 00:03:11.175
of learning about the human mind.

00:03:11.175 --> 00:03:14.079
Some people, you know, we
could work on them for years

00:03:14.079 --> 00:03:16.313
and we can't get rid of
the obsessive thoughts.

00:03:16.313 --> 00:03:18.246
It's always a challenge to figure out

00:03:18.246 --> 00:03:19.883
how to take care of them.

00:03:19.883 --> 00:03:22.633
(dramatic music)

